"COSPLAY Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/cosplay)\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/cosplay\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 219\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: [Input: Search Dictionary]\nL3: \nL4:   * 【2†Games】\nL5:   * 【3†Word of the Day】\nL6:   * 【4†Grammar】\nL7:   * 【5†Word Finder】\nL8:   * 【6†Slang】\nL9:   * 【7†Newsletters】\nL10:   * 【8†Wordplay】\nL11:   * 【9†Rhymes】\nL12:   * 【10†Thesaurus】\nL13:   * 【11†Join MWU†unabridged.merriam-webster.com】\nL14:   * More\nL15:     * 【2†Games】\nL16:     * 【3†Word of the Day】\nL17:     * 【4†Grammar】\nL18:     * 【8†Wordplay】\nL19:     * 【6†Slang】\nL20:     * 【9†Rhymes】\nL21:     * 【5†Word Finder】\nL22:     * 【7†Newsletters】\nL23:     * 【10†Thesaurus】\nL24:     * 【11†Join MWU†unabridged.merriam-webster.com】\nL25:     * Shop\nL26:     * 【12†Books†shop.merriam-webster.com】\nL27:     * 【13†Merch†merriamwebster.threadless.com】\nL28: \nL29: 【14†Dictionary】 Definition\nL30: \nL31:   * # cosplay\nL32: \nL33: 1 of 2\nL34: ## 【15†noun】\nL35: \nL36: cos·​play 【16†ˈkäz-ˌplā 】\nL37: \nL38: ˈkäs-\nL39: \nL40: plural cosplays\nL41: \nL42: : the activity or practice of dressing up as a character from a work of fiction (such as a comic book, video game, or television show)\nL43: In cosplay, a good costume is as much about creativity with materials and construction as it is about the outcome. So while some people spend hundreds or thousands of dollars on custom outfits for cons, many more invest in time, using cheap materials and household items to transform themselves into characters of superhuman, mythical proportions.—Jamal Jordan\nL44: \nL45: —often used before another noun\nL46: \nL47: cosplay fans\nL48: \nL49: Festivities would not be complete without a cosplay competition.—Charlotte Bray\nL50: Quiz: If you spend months preparing an elaborate cosplay for a big event, what is a common outcome?\nL51: \nL52: cosplay\nL53: \nL54: 2 of 2\nL55: ## 【17†verb】\nL56: \nL57: cosplayed; cosplaying; cosplays\nL58: \nL59: 1\nL60: \nL61: 【18†intransitive】 : to engage in 【19†cosplay】\nL62: \nL63: cosplaying as anime characters\nL64: \nL65: The positive reaction he received from fellow conventioneers felt so good that he's been cosplaying ever since.—Pam Kragen\nL66: \nL67: 2\nL68: \nL69: 【20†transitive】 : to dress up as (a fictional character) in cosplay\nL70: She said she has cosplayed other women in science fiction or fantasy, but Wonder Woman is the first woman superhero, and one of the earliest representations of a strong, capable woman she saw growing up.—Rebecca White\nL71: \nL72: cosplaying noun\nL73: \nL74: Cosplaying is a big, colorful part of Tokyo in Tulsa and it's safe to say that, other than Halloween, Broken Arrow has never been populated by this many costumed characters. —Jimmie Tramel\nL75: \nL76: a cosplaying event\nL77: \nL78: 【21†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】\nL79: ## Did you know?\nL80: If you enjoy cosplaying as your favorite 【22†anime】 character (say, Nezuko Kamada from Demon Slayer or Luffy from One Piece), you’ve got yourself a special, lexicographical 【23†twofer】 of words that were borrowed from English into Japanese and then back into English. In Japanese, anime is short for animēshiyon, which comes from the English word 【24†animation】, referring to an animated cartoon.\nL81: Japanese users similarly took the English words 【25†costume】 and play (as in 【26†role-play†bit.ly】) and combined them into the word kosuchūmupurē, or kosupure for short, which was reborrowed into English as cosplay, first as a noun, and later as a verb.\nL82: It's not required that one choose an anime character to cosplay, however—any fictional character will do, and probably has done! People are even starting to use cosplay figuratively to mean “to pretend to be,” as in “her 【27†chiweenie】 likes to cosplay as a much bigger dog whenever they visit the dog park.”\nL83: ## Examples of cosplay in a Sentence\nL84: \nL85: Recent Examples on the Web\nL86: \nL87: Examples are automatically compiled from online sources to show current usage. 【14†Read More】 Opinions expressed in the examples do not represent those of Merriam-Webster or its editors. 【28†Send us feedback†docs.google.com】.\nL88: \nL89: Noun\nL90: Early on, her costumes evolved from classic (a dominatrix the first year, a vampire in 2005) to more complex (a skinless person in 2011) to complete cosplay. — José Criales-Unzueta, Vanity Fair, 28 Oct. 2026 Manga Day Festival Enjoy a free day of manga, anime and sequential art with a welcome parade, K-pop Demon singalong, a cosplay runway showcase, free swag bags to the first 300 preregistered arrivals, plus drawing and masterclasses. — Mary Caldwell, AJC.com, 27 Oct. 2026\nL91: \nL92: Verb\nL93: But for a few hours every year, the VMA bash is when MTV decides to cosplay as MTV for a night—and the Nineties glow of this year’s model made that clearer than ever. — Rob Sheffield, Rolling Stone, 28 Sep. 2026 Where to Stay Within walking distance of the North Ferry, The Boathouse is the ultimate home base for anyone wishing to cosplay as a Shelter Island homeowner for the weekend. — Sylvie Florman, Vogue, 12 Aug. 2026 【29†See All Example Sentences for cosplay】\nL94: ## Word History\nL95: \nL96: Etymology\nL97: \nL98: Noun\nL99: \nL100: 【30†cos(tume) entry 1】 + 【31†play entry 2】, after Japanese kosupure, short for kosuchūmupurē, borrowed from English costume + (role) play\nL101: Note: The Japanese word was allegedly introduced in print by the Japanese publisher and media producer Noboyuki Takahashi (born 1957) in the June, 1983 issue of the magazine Mai Anime/My Anime (see Brian Ashcraft and Luke Plunkett, Cosplay World [Prestel, 2014], pp. 18-20; the authors quote from an interview with Takahashi and reproduce pages from the magazine).\nL102: \nL103: Verb\nL104: \nL105: derivative of 【19†cosplay entry 1】\nL106: \nL107: First Known Use\nL108: \nL109: Noun\nL110: \nL111: 1993, in the meaning defined 【19†above】\nL112: \nL113: Verb\nL114: 1993, in the meaning defined at 【32†sense 1】\nL115: \nL116: Time Traveler\nL117: \nL118: The first known use of cosplay was in 1993\nL119: \nL120: 【33†See more words from the same year】\nL121: ## Browse Nearby Words\nL122: \nL123: 【34†cospecific】\nL124: \nL125: cosplay\nL126: \nL127: 【35†cosplayer】\nL128: \nL129: 【36†See all Nearby Words】\nL130: \nL131: ## Articles Related to cosplay\nL132: \nL133: 【37†The Words of the Week - Jul. 31 Dictionary lookups from law, rodents, and ninepins】 【38†Which Came First? \"Jeggings\" or \"mom jeans\"? \"Chillax\" or \"dumpster fire\"?】 【39†On 'Kawaii' and the Power of Cute Let's put a face on it.】\nL134: \nL135: ## Podcast\nL136: ## Cite this Entry\nL137: \nL138: Style\nL139: \nL140: [Select]\nL141: \nL142: “Cosplay.” Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary, Merriam-Webster, https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/cosplay. Accessed 2 Nov. 2026.\nL143: \nL144: 【40†Image†prod.cdn-mw.com】Copy Citation\nL145: \nL146: Love words? Need even more definitions?\nL147: \nL148: Subscribe to America's largest dictionary and get thousands more definitions and advanced search—ad free!\nL149: \nL150: Last Updated: 29 Oct 2026 - Updated example sentences\nL151: \nL152: ## More from Merriam-Webster\nL153: \nL154: #### 【3†Word of the Day】\nL155: #### 【3†satiate】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nypl.org/blog/2019/04/04/beginners-guide-mecha-manga-anime\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.nypl.org/blog/2019/04/04/beginners-guide-mecha-manga-anime: (403) Forbidden--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMighty Atom｜MANGA｜TEZUKA OSAMU OFFICIAL (https://tezukaosamu.net/en/manga/291.html)\nciteturn5view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://tezukaosamu.net/en/manga/291.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 200\nL0:   *   *   *   *   * 【0†Ranking】\nL1:   * 【1†Alphabetical Order】\nL2:   * 【2†Chronology】\nL3: \nL4: 【3†Image】\nL5: \nL6: 【4†Image】\nL7: \nL8: Frontispiece/Daisan no Majutushi no Maki/Shonen/Kobunsha/1962\nL9: \nL10: 【5†Story】\nL11: \nL12: 【6†Description】\nL13: \nL14: 【7†Characters】\nL15: \nL16: 【8†Comment by Author】\nL17: ## Story\nL18: \nL19: This is a Sci-Fi manga set in the 21st century, featuring Astro Boy, a robot hero with 100,000 horse power.\nL20: \nL21: 【9†Image】\nL22: \nL23: 【10†Ambassador Atom】/1951\nL24: On April 7, 2003, Dr. Tenma, Director of Science Ministry, makes up a robot which looks exactly like Tobio, his late son Tobio involved in a traffic accident, by utilizing all the knowledge and power of the ministry. He loves the robot just like his real son, but gradually gets irritated at the robot as it does not grow up like a human. Finally, he sells out the robot to a circus. At the circus, the robot, newly named Astro, is forced to work.\nL25: Soon later, so-called \"robot right\" is approved thanks to the effort of Dr. Ochanomizu, new Director of Science Ministry. The right brings freedom to Astro, too. Astro comes to live with his robot parents made by Dr. Ochanomizu in the suburbs and go to Ochanomizu elementary school. Yet Astro is a super hero with 100,000 horse power. He confronts evils bravely whenever he is needed.\nL26: 【9†Image】\nL27: \nL28: 【10†Ambassador Atom】/　1951\nL29: \nL30: 【11†Image】\nL31: ## Description\nL32: \nL33: Serialized in Shonen (Kobunsha) (1952/04-1968/03)\nL34: The character Astro Boy and the manga work Astro Boy are both loved by a lot of celebrities, artists and cultural figures, considered as the symbol of Tezuka's clear-sightedness and conscience and often used in a figurative sense, such as \"scientists specializing in robot engineering hoping to make robots like Astro Boy\" or \"a town looks like a futuristic city appearing in Astro Boy\". Dr. Ochanomizu is also used in a figurative sense, too, such as \"If asked to name one scientist, I would name Dr.\nL35: Ochanomizu.\" Furthermore, Astro Boy is often picked up as a theme for art, a symbol of science technology or a motif of manga. Thus, Astro Boy has been widely disseminated among people.\nL36: 【12†Image】\nL37: \nL38: 【13†Image】\nL39: Originally, Astro Boy was a robot boy appearing in Ambassador Atom as one of the side characters, and the manga Astro Boy was a new manga series following Ambassador Atom, featuring the robot as the main character. The work gained popularity among people, becoming one of the most famous works by Tezuka Osamu. In 1963, the work was turned into TV animation series, which was the first commercial animation produced by Mushi Production established by Tezuka as well as the first Japanese TV animation series.\nL40: The series consisting of 30 minutes episodes was broadcast every week, initiating the first TV animation boom in Japan.\nL41: 【13†Image】\nL42: ## Characters\nL43: \nL44: 【14†Image】\nL45: \nL46: Astro Boy\nL47: \nL48: ### Astro Boy\nL49: \nL50: He is a robot boy with 100,000 horsepower and seven special abilities. Although he was originally made, modeled on Dr. Tenma's late son, Tobio, he is sold to a circus and re-named Astro Boy by the circus manager. With his super abilities, he solves several problems to save the Earth.\nL51: 【15†>Character/Astro Boy】\nL52: \nL53: 【14†Image】\nL54: \nL55: Astro Boy\nL56: \nL57: 【16†Image】\nL58: \nL59: Dr. Ochanomizu\nL60: \nL61: 【17†Image】\nL62: \nL63: Dr. Tenma\nL64: ### Dr. Ochanomizu\nL65: \nL66: He is Director of Science Ministry sympathetic to robots, believing they also have the rights as humans do. One day, Dr. Ochanomizu finds Astro working at the circus and decides to become his guardian, taking him along to his home. He also makes Uran and Astro's parents for him.\nL67: 【18†>Character/Dr. Ochanomizu】\nL68: ### Dr. Tenma\nL69: \nL70: He is a genius robot engineer and the creator of Astro Boy. He sometimes goes to the extreme, and sells Astro out to the circus, get irritated that he does not grow up as humans do.\nL71: 【19†>Character/Dr. Tenma】\nL72: \nL73: 【20†Image】\nL74: \nL75: Uran\nL76: \nL77: 【21†Image】\nL78: \nL79: Cobalt\nL80: \nL81: ### Uran\nL82: \nL83: She is a robot girl made as Astro's younger sister. She is tomboy but cares about her brother very much.\nL84: 【22†>Character/Uran】\nL85: ### Cobalt\nL86: \nL87: He is a robot boy made as Astro's younger brother. He is kind a little easygoing and not good at detailed work. He is equipped with a function to resonate with Astro.\nL88: \nL89: 【23†Image】\nL90: \nL91: Inspector Tawashi\nL92: \nL93: 【24†Image】\nL94: \nL95: Inspector Nakamura\nL96: \nL97: ### Inspector Tawashi\nL98: \nL99: He is a detective standing in opposition to robots. He doubts a robot as a criminal whenever incidents occur. Astro has also been suspected several times. But he is basically a dedicated detective.\nL100: ### Inspector Nakamura\nL101: \nL102: He is a detective sympathetic to robots under Inspector Tawashi's command.\nL103: \nL104: 【25†Image】\nL105: \nL106: Higeoyaji\nL107: \nL108: 【26†Image】\nL109: \nL110: Tamao\nL111: \nL112: ### Higeoyaji\nL113: \nL114: He is a teacher at Astro's school, also a private detective.\nL115: 【27†>Character/Higeoyaji】\nL116: \nL117: ### Tamao\nL118: \nL119: He is Astro's classmate, wearing big eye glasses and a hat. He cares about his friends and gets panic easily.\nL120: \nL121: 【28†Image】\nL122: \nL123: Kenichi\nL124: \nL125: 【29†Image】\nL126: \nL127: Shibugaki\nL128: ### Kenichi\nL129: \nL130: He is Astro's classmate. He is clever and has a strong sense of justice, also good at organizing the class.\nL131: 【30†>Character/Kenichi】\nL132: \nL133: ### Shibugaki\nL134: \nL135: He is Astro's classmate. He is rough and sometimes mean, but somehow maintains a good relationship with Astro.\nL136: ## Comment by Author\nL137: \nL138: 【31†Image】\nL139: \nL140: Excerpt from \"Atom to Watashi\" (I and Astro Boy) in Astro Boy, The Science of Robots published by Kobunsha\nL141: It's been almost fifteen years since I began writing Astro Boy. Now Astro Boy is cited as my representative work, but I never thought it lasted such a long time when I started it.\nL142: At the beginning of the serialization, no artificial satellite existed, no one knew who Gagarin was, and even TV was nothing more than a dream. And manga was annoyed by people, considered as illusional things to read which brought bad influence to the society.\nL143: But all those illusions have come to life now. Accordingly, I was put in a situation that I had to redraw some of the manuscripts.\nL144: Today, my young readers have already known a rocket to the moon and a computerized brain as common knowledge, saying \"A vacuum tube in Astro's computerized brain? It's out of fashion. Even in a calculator, transistors or diodes are used\". In their imagination, quite advanced robots equipped with computerized brains and atomic engines just like Astro Boy have already been flying, helping humans in daily life. (omitted)\nL145: 【31†Image】\nL146: \nL147: 【32†Image】\nL148: \nL149: 【10†Ambassador Atom】\nL150: \nL151: 【33†Image】\nL152: \nL153: Astro giving a resent to his parents\nL154: \nL155: (Excerpt form Boku wa Mangaka published by Manichi Newspaper)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nanime - Terminology | Japanese with Anime (https://www.japanesewithanime.com/2016/08/anime.html?m=0)\nciteturn5view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.japanesewithanime.com/2016/08/anime.html?m=0\",\"lineno\":590}); Total lines: 876\nL530: Action and fighting series, scenes, can be very costly to animate compared to adaptations of comedy 【126†yonkoma ４コマ】 where all characters do is talk to each other. A low budget approach to action scenes is strategically structuring cuts to avoid drawing too much movement.\nL531: Where a high budget would draw a character punching another and them flying to the wall in a single cut, for example, a low budget series could start with the punch, but before it landed, switch to the attacked character's face in pain, and then switch to them flying, all in order to reduce the complexity of the animation.\nL532: ### BGM\nL533: \nL534: The term BGM is abbreviation of BackGround Music, バックグラウンドミュージック. In native Japanese: haikei ongaku 背景音楽.\nL535: \nL536: ### Fanservice\nL537: \nL538: The term 【127†fanservice】 refers to anything added for fans, but typically suggestive scenes, poses, clothing, tropes, and so on added as a bonus in a series. Common examples include: shower scenes, bath scenes, beach episodes, hot springs episodes, clothing randomly getting torn apart, among others.\nL539: ### Stitch\nL540: \nL541: A stitch is a large unofficial image created by putting together frames of a panning shot from an anime.\nL542: \nL543: Often, a large illustration is drawn for an anime that will be shown zoomed in, to a level it won't fit the screen, so the \"camera\" will pan, from bottom to top, for example, to show the whole illustration.\nL544: \nL545: A stitch is taking frames of this panning shot and editing them together to form the large illustration, so you can see the whole thing at once, instead of a panning shot.\nL546: Painting: Mona Lisa, by Leonardo da Vinci (in Public Domain)\nL547: Source: 【17†japanesewithanime.com】 (【18†CC BY-SA 4.0†creativecommons.org】)\nL548: \nL549: For reference, an example of a stitched image:\nL550: \nL551: Character: Touwa Erio 藤和エリオ\nL552: Anime: Denpa Onna to Seishun Otoko 電波女と青春男 (Episode 1, Stitch)\nL553: \nL554:   * Context: a girl 【128†wrapped into a blanket like a burrito】 sits on a zabuton 座布団 in 【129†seiza 正座】 position.\nL555: ### Sequel Names\nL556: \nL557: For reference, some common patterns in titles of anime sequel:\nL558: #### Number\nL559: \nL560: The 【113†counter†media.japanesewithanime.com】 ~ki ～期 is used to count anime seasons in Japanese.\nL561: \nL562:   * ikki\nL563: １期\nL564: Season one.\nL565:   * ni-ki\nL566: ２期\nL567: Season two.\nL568: \nL569: If you see ni-ki in the title of an anime, that means it's the season two of that anime.\nL570: \nL571:   * Ansatsu Kyoushitsu Dai-ni-ki\nL572: 暗殺教室　第２期\nL573: Assassination Classroom Second Season.\nL574: \nL575: Some anime just use the 【130†ni 二】, \"two.\" For example:\nL576: \nL577:   * Baka to Test to Shoukanjuu Ni'!\nL578: バカとテストと召喚獣　にっ！\nL579: Baka and Test and Summoned-Beasts Two.\nL580: Some anime use tsuu ツー, the 【84†katakanization of the English number】 \"two.\"\nL581: \nL582:   * Toaru Majutsu no Indekkusu Tsuu\nL583: とある魔術の禁書目録＜インデックス＞ＩＩ\nL584: Toaru Majutsu no Index Two.\nL585:   * Kiratto Puri-chan Shiizon Tsuu\nL586: キラッとプリ☆チャン　シーズン２\nL587: Kiratto Pri-chan Season Two.\nL588: #### ~hen ～編\nL589: \nL590: The suffix ~hen ～編, or ～篇, means \"edition,\" as in a \"compilation\" of content. It's used in titles of anime seasons that adapt an arc as a whole. For example:\nL591: \nL592:   * Owari no Serafu: Nagoya Kessen-hen\nL593: 終わりのセラフ　名古屋決戦編\nL594: Seraph of the End: Nagoya's Decisive Battle Arc.\nL595:   * Kami nomi zo Shiru Sekai: Megami-hen\nL596: 神のみぞ知るセカイ 女神篇\nL597: The World Only God Knows: Goddess Arc.\nL598: \nL599: A kanketsu-hen 完結編 is specifically the \"concluding part\" of a series, i.e. the \"final arc.\"\nL600:   * Inuyasha: Kanketsu-hen\nL601: 犬夜叉　完結編\nL602: Inuyasha: Final arc.\nL603:   * Saiki Kusuo no Sainan: Kanketsu-hen\nL604: 斉木楠雄のΨ難　完結編\nL605: Saiku Kusuo's Disaster: Final Arc.\nL606: #### zoku 続\nL607: \nL608: The word 【131†zoku 続】 means \"continuation,\" tudzuki 続き, sometimes seen as zoku-hen 続編, \"continuation arc,\" i.e. \"the sequel.\"\nL609: \nL610:   * Zoku Natsume Yuujinchou\nL611: 続　夏目友人帳\nL612: Continuation: Natsume's Book of Friends.\nL613:   * Koodo Giasu Hangyaku no Ruruushu Zoku-hen\nL614: コードギアス　反逆のルルーシュ　続編\nL615: Code Geass: LeLouch of the Revolution - The Sequel.\nL616: #### Extra Exclamation Marks\nL617: \nL618: Some anime sequels have an extra exclamation mark or some other unusual punctuation instead of a word that makes sense like \"season 2\" or \"sequel.\"\nL619: \nL620: For reference, some examples:^{(【132†rrr-lang.hatenablog.com】)}\nL621: Season 1  | Year\nL622: --- | ---\nL623: Himawari'!\nL624: ひまわりっ！  | 2006-04.\nL625: Himawari'!!\nL626: ひまわりっ！！  | 2007-01.\nL627: Shugo Kyara!\nL628: しゅごキャラ！  | 2007-10.\nL629: Shugo Kyara!! Doki'\nL630: しゅごキャラ！！どきっ  | 2008-10.\nL631: Shugo Kyara!!! Dokki Doki\nL632: しゅごキャラ！！！どっきどき  | 2009-10.\nL633: Hayate no Gotoku!\nL634: ハヤテのごとく！  | 2007-04.\nL635: Hayate no Gotoku!!\nL636: ハヤテのごとく！！  | 2009-04.\nL637: K-on!\nL638: けいおん！  | 2009-04.\nL639: K-on!!\nL640: けいおん！！  | 2010-04.\nL641: Working!!  | 2010-04.\nL642: Working'!!  | 2011-10.\nL643: Working!!!  | 2015-07.\nL644: Shinryaku! Ika Musume\nL645: 侵略！イカ娘  | 2010-10.\nL646: Shinryaku!? Ika Musume\nL647: 侵略！？イカ娘  | 2011-09.\nL648: Yuru Yuri\nL649: ゆるゆり  | 2011-07.\nL650: Yuru Yuri♪♪\nL651: ゆるゆり♪♪  | 2012-07.\nL652: Yuri Yuri,\nL653: ゆるゆり、  | 2019-09.\nL654: PUCHIM@S!\nL655: ぷちます！  | 2013-01.\nL656: PUCHIM@S!!\nL657: ぷちます！！  | 2014-04.\nL658: Gochuumon wa Usagi desu ka?\nL659: ご注文はうさぎですか？  | 2014-04.\nL660: Gochuumon wa Usagi desu ka??\nL661: ご注文はうさぎですか？？  | 2015-10.\nL662: New Game!  | 2016-07.\nL663: New Game!!  | 2017-07.\nL664: Bokutachi wa Benkyou ga Dekinai\nL665: ぼくたちは勉強ができない  | 2019-04.\nL666: Bokutachi wa Benkyou ga Dekinai!\nL667: ぼくたちは勉強ができない！  | 2019-10.\nL668: #### Gekijouban\nL669: \nL670: A gekijouban 劇場版 is a \"theatrical version,\" i.e. an anime movie, shown on movie theaters, rather than on TV.\nL671: \nL672: It's sometimes the case that the start of a series is adapted as a TV anime and a later arc is adapted as a movie instead. For reference, some examples:\nL673:   * Gekijouban: Kimetsu no Yaiba: Mugen Ressha-hen\nL674: 劇場版　鬼滅の刃　無限列車編\nL675: Theatrical Version: Demon Slayer: Infinite Train Arc.\nL676:   * Gekijouban: Vaioretto Evaagaaden\nL677: 劇場版 ヴァイオレット・エヴァーガーデン\nL678: Theatrical Version: Violet Evergarden.\nL679:   * Gekijouban: Soodoaato Onrain -Oodhinaru Sukeeru-\nL680: 劇場版　ソードアート・オンライン -オーディナル・スケール-\nL681: Theatrical Version: Sword Art Online -Ordinal Scale-.\nL682: ### Seiyuu\nL683: \nL684: The word seiyuu 声優 means \"voice actor,\" and a voice actor is someone who does the voice of an anime character.\nL685: ### Sakuga\nL686: \nL687: In Japanese, sakuga 作画, meaning literally \"working picture,\" refers to the work done to animate drawings in an anime. In English, sakuga is typically used to refer to an anime that looks well-animated, moving smoothly or beautifully.\nL688: \nL689:   * genga\nL690: 原画\nL691: \"Origin picture.\"\nL692: Key animation, which serves as the base, i.e. \"origin,\" upon which the anime is animated.\nL693:   * douga\nL694: 動画\nL695: \"Moving picture.\"\nL696: In-between animation, the process of drawing frames between the key frames of key animation.\nL697: The combination of the two steps above is called sakuga 作画 in Japanese.^{(【133†vantan-game.com】)}\nL698: \nL699: Character: Anrakutei Kukuru 暗落亭苦来\nL700: Anime: Joshiraku じょしらく (Episode 1)\nL701: \nL702:   * Context: that's a lot of sakuga.\nL703:   * nurunuru ugoku\nL704: ヌルヌル動く\nL705: To move smoothly.\nL706: (【134†reduplicated】 【10†mimetic word】.)\nL707: \nL708: Observe that the example above is a single cut.\nL709: \nL710: A complete anime has steps besides sakuga, since anime isn't just animating characters, e.g.:\nL711:   * saishiki\nL712: 彩色\nL713: Coloring, as in adding colors to the sakuga, which, although drawn with colored lines, doesn't have colored fills.\nL714:   * haikei\nL715: 背景\nL716: Background, as what's drawn behind the characters, that doesn't move.\nL717:   * satsuei\nL718: 撮影\nL719: Filming, putting the colored frames, background, etc., together into a video.\nL720: ### Anison\nL721: \nL722: An anison アニソン, standing for anime song, アニメソング, refers to the songs played in the OP and ED of an anime, as well as other songs that may show up in key points in a series, or just anime-esque songs in general.^{(【135†dic.nicovideo.jp】)}\nL723: ### Key Visual\nL724: \nL725: In Japanese, a key visual キービジュアル is a main image of used to market something in a public website, pamphlet, etc. For anime, a key visual generally features an image of the its characters.^{(【136†oca.ac.jp】)}\nL726: \nL727: For the record, this term has nothing to do with key animation. Presumably, it's called \"key visual\" because it's \"key\" as in \"main\" and \"visual\" as in \"image.\"\nL728: \nL729: Just like releasing a PV, releasing a key visual means you're releasing information about a project.\nL730: Anime: Your Name., Kimi no Na wa. 君の名は。\nL731: Image source: 【137†kiminona.com†www.kiminona.com】, accessed 2021-05-15.\nL732: \nL733:   * Context: the key visual used to market Your Name.\nL734:   * From this, you can already tell what the main characters look like.\nL735: ### Subs\nL736: \nL737: The subs are subtitles, the text containing the English translation of the Japanese dialogue in an anime.\nL738: \nL739: #### Fansub\nL740: \nL741: A fansub is an unofficial translation of an anime, with English subtitles. A fan who subtitles anime is a fansubber. Given how this can be a lot of work, translating, typesetting, quality-checking, etc., fansubbing is typically done by several people working as a fansub group.\nL742: #### Softsub\nL743: \nL744: A softsub is a subtitle that can be toggled on and off. There are two types: external files (e.g. in the `.srt` extension) that can be loaded into a video player, and subtitle tracks embedded into the video file.\nL745: \nL746: For the later, typically the Matroska (`.mkv`) video container format is used, as it allows a video file to contain multiple audio and subtitle tracks.\nL747: #### Hardsub\nL748: \nL749: A hardsub is a subtitle that can't be toggled off. The text is edited into the video frames and the whole video has to be re-encoded, which is the video variant of adding a text to a `.jpg` image and saving it as `.jpg` again.\nL750: \nL751: Hardsubs are used mainly because of the lack of support for softsubs.\n"
